3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader mRNA contains four regions capable of alternative base-pairing. Under Trp-replete conditions the ribosome translates trpL to its stop codon, thereby covering region 2 and freeing region 3 to pair with region 4 and form the terminator hairpin.  

A mutation that prevents region 1 from pairing with region 2 leaves region 2 free to pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed. Formation of the 2-3 antiterminator sequesters region 3, so the 3-4 terminator cannot form even when the ribosome is covering region 2. Consequently transcription continues through the operon.  

The other options either favor terminator formation (B), alter termination efficiency without blocking the stem-loop itself (C), or affect promoter strength or peptide abundance without changing hairpin choice (D, E).}

5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader transcript contains four segments that can pair in alternative structures: 1-2 (pause), 2-3 (antiterminator), or 3-4 (intrinsic terminator). Under high tryptophan the ribosome translates the leader peptide without stalling, thereby covering segment 2 when segment 3 is transcribed; this blocks 2-3 pairing and allows the 3-4 terminator to form, halting transcription.  

A mutation that prevents segment 1 from pairing with segment 2 leaves segment 2 free to base-pair with segment 3 as soon as segment 3 appears. Consequently the antiterminator forms constitutively, the 3-4 stem-loop never forms, and transcription continues even when tryptophan is abundant.  

The other options either favor terminator formation (B), strengthen the terminator (C), or reduce overall transcription (E); none of them selectively block 3-4 pairing under repressing conditions.}

